 "productionNotes": "No production total is asserted for this page, because its scope is the US-market car and no source consulted publishes a US total. The global figure is well agreed: Hagerty (December 2020) and Wikipedia both give 21,529,464 Type 1s built worldwide, the last on July 30, 2003 at Puebla, Mexico, and Volkswagen's US newsroom rounds it to 21.5 million first-generation cars sold. That is a model-line total spanning 1945 to 2003 and several continents, so it cannot stand in for the 1949-1979 US car. Hagerty puts German production at around 15.8 million, and Wikipedia records 11,916,519 built at Wolfsburg alone before the line there closed on July 1, 1974. US sales appear only as milestones in Hagerty's 2022 buyer's guide: two cars in 1949, 160 in 1950, 320 in 1951, roughly 9,000 in 1954, more than 100,000 in 1960 and about three times that by the middle of the decade. No source consulted splits production or US sales by body, so the number of US Super Beetles or US convertibles is not stated here. One genuine disagreement concerns the end of the line: Volkswagen's own US heritage page says the last air-cooled Beetle rolled off an assembly line in South America in 2003, while Hagerty, in two separate articles, and Wikipedia place the final car at Puebla, Mexico. US pricing is thin. Hagerty gives $1,280 for a standard car in 1950 with about $200 more for the Deluxe, and Volkswagen gives a price of around $1,600 in the early-to-mid 1950s. No verified US list price by model year was found for the 1960s and 1970s cars in the sources consulted, and none is given.",
